From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 18:05:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: t8132-j604: Drop the boot framebuffer's
 power domains

t8132-jxxx.dtsi gives the boot framebuffer the power domains
<&ps_disp_fe>, <&ps_dptx_phy>. The J604 PMGR policy, which follows
t8132-j713-pmgr.dtsi, makes ps_disp_fe firmware-owned and disables
ps_dptx_phy, so the DPTX PHY domain is never registered. simpledrm then
waits for that domain until the deferred-probe timeout. After the
timeout, pm_domain_attach_by_id(1) fails with -110 and simpledrm
registers about 11 s into boot. When the DCP driver is enabled, it has
by then already removed the conflicting firmware framebuffers, so a
second, stale DRM card remains next to it.

The J713 board file has the same PMGR policy, and it deletes this
property for the same reason (commit 332eea1e9005 ("arm64: apple:
activate J713 with its PMGR ownership policy")): iBoot and the DCP set up
and keep the display blocks powered, and Linux does not yet own the
T8132 display power sequence. Do the same on the J604.

The J604 Apple device tree (macOS 26B123 and 25G83) describes the same
DPTX_PHY PMGR device (id 102, no parent) as the J713's, so this changes
only what Linux claims, not which hardware is described. ps_dptx_phy and
ps_disp_fe keep their nodes and their policy status.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
index ba972a558f..5ea809ec87 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
@@ -771,6 +771,19 @@
 	};
 };
 
+/*
+ * As on the J713: iBoot and the DCP set up and keep the display blocks
+ * powered. Linux does not yet own the T8132 display power sequence, and
+ * t8132-j604-pmgr.dtsi keeps ps_disp_fe firmware-owned and ps_dptx_phy
+ * unregistered. The boot framebuffer therefore has no domain for Linux to
+ * hold on. Leaving ps_dptx_phy in its power-domains would hold simpledrm
+ * back until the deferred-probe timeout, and then it would register after
+ * the DCP driver has already taken over the display.
+ */
+&framebuffer0 {
+	/delete-property/ power-domains;
+};
+
 #include "t8132-j604-gpu.dtsi"
 
 /*
